# What goes to a connection.
#
# Either bytes that are already what should be written, or a message the
# connection has to say in its own protocol.
#
# The queue rendering a message was fine while there was one protocol. There
# are two now — RillMQ's own and AMQP — and a delivery in AMQP carries a tag
# the queue could not know, because the tag counts deliveries on that
# connection rather than messages in that queue. So the queue says what
# happened and the connection says it.
type Out
  OFrame(text: Str)
  # `props` is whatever a client put in the message's header, kept exactly as
  # it sent it. RillMQ has no use for any of it and hands it back byte for
  # byte, which is the only way `reply-to` and `correlation-id` can mean on the
  # way out what they meant on the way in.
  # `sub` is which subscriber the queue chose. A connection may have several
  # consumers, on several channels, and the frame has to go back on the one
  # that asked: guessing from the queue's name was right only while a
  # connection could have one consumer per queue.
  ODeliver(queue: Str, id: Int, body: Str, props: Str, sub: Int)
  # A message that is now on the disk. RillMQ's own protocol says `+OK <id>`;
  # AMQP says `Basic.Ack` and only when the client asked for confirms. Both
  # are the same event, so the event is what travels.
  OPublished(queue: Str, id: Int)
  # A message that matched nothing, on its way back to whoever sent it. Only a
  # publisher that asked for it with `mandatory` ever sees one: without that
  # flag an unroutable message is dropped, which is what AMQP says and what
  # every broker does, and the flag is how a publisher says it would rather
  # know.
  # `chan` travels with it because the answer comes back long after the
  # publish did, by which time the connection has moved on and no longer knows
  # which channel asked. A message carrying where it is going is the only kind
  # that arrives.
  ONoRoute(exchange: Str, key: Str, body: Str, props: Str, chan: Int)
  # The queue this subscriber was reading has gone. AMQP calls it
  # `Basic.Cancel` from the server, and a consumer that is not told is a
  # consumer waiting on something that will never speak again.
  OCancelled(queue: Str, sub: Int)
